Design lineage
================================================================

No code, file, or algorithm from any third-party project is included in,
or was ported into, this repository. The items below are architectural
ideas that shaped this design; each is implemented independently in this
project's own stack (Python / FastAPI / Pydantic / SQLite over markdown
files), verified against the cited upstream source rather than copied
from it.

Verified: a symbol search across this repository's source for
buzz-specific identifiers (pg_advisory_lock, catch_unwind, AuditError,
RelayMessage, axum::, tokio::, kind_u32, is_gift_wrap, GENESIS_HASH, and
related names) returns zero matches. The two codebases share no function,
struct, or dependency; the ideas below were read, tested against this
project's own data, and re-implemented from scratch in a different
language and a different storage model.

--- block/buzz (https://github.com/block/buzz), Apache License 2.0 ---

- Relay owns the log, serves queries.
  Follows buzz-relay's architecture (ARCHITECTURE.md: "The relay is the
  single source of truth"): llminbox is a derived index sitting in
  front of the markdown ledgers it serves, never the record of truth
  itself. If it dies, nothing is blocked — see README, falsifier T5.

- Typed, addressed events.
  The append contract's mandatory `tipo` (type) and `to` (recipients)
  fields follow the same shape as buzz's Nostr `kind` + `#p`/`#h` tags:
  every event declares what it is and who it is for. Independently
  implemented in Python/Pydantic over markdown headers; nothing was
  ported from buzz's Rust/Nostr code.

- Validate at indexing time, not only at the write edge.
  Idea taken from buzz-relay (handlers/event.rs:655-660 and
  ingest.rs:1525), which repeats the same duplicate-pubkey check in two
  handlers on purpose, because one path skips the main pipeline. Same
  shape here, on a worse ratio: in this project's own deployment, raw
  `>>` appends outnumber the validated `POST /append` path roughly 8 to
  1. If the only validator were the write path, most real traffic would
  enter unchecked. `GET /lint` validates what's indexed, regardless of
  how it was written.

- A hash chain — tried, measured, and retired.
  The first working version of this project's integrity check was
  patterned after buzz-audit (ARCHITECTURE.md, "buzz-audit — Hash-Chain
  Audit Log"): prev_hash chaining, an all-zero genesis, and a
  verify_chain() that recomputes and compares. Measured against this
  project's actual shape — one append-only markdown file with several
  independent writers, not a single-writer table under an advisory lock
  — the chain was found inert here: the same code computed both sides of
  the comparison, so it always matched itself and could only ever catch
  corruption of its own database. It was also found incorrect by design
  once tested against a real multi-writer scenario: a git merge preserves
  every entry but not their order, and a chain keyed by position reports
  that as mass tampering. It was retired in favor of git's own commit
  chain plus a simpler, orthogonal check this project still runs: does an
  append-only file ever lose an entry it once had. See
  docs/DESIGN-NOTES.es.md for the full account, including the two
  measurement mistakes that produced false confidence along the way.

No further code, structures, or algorithms from block/buzz appear in
this repository.


================================================================
Third-party dependencies
================================================================

This project depends at runtime on FastAPI, Pydantic and Uvicorn,
installed via pip at the exact transitive versions and artefact hashes in
requirements.lock. They are not vendored into this repository.

Their licences, and those of their transitive dependencies, are NOT
asserted here. An earlier version of this file stated that all of them
were MIT, BSD-3-Clause or PSF-2.0 and that none required reproduction of
its licence text. That conclusion was never measured, and it contradicted
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, which records the inventory as incomplete. It has
been withdrawn rather than reworded: a licence conclusion that nobody ran
is not made true by being cautiously phrased.

What is measured, and what is still missing, is recorded in
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. That file is the only place in this repository
where a third-party licence is stated, and it states none that was not
read from the package itself.

The container image is built in two stages, and the second copies the
compiled web bundle out of the first. The image therefore redistributes both
the JavaScript packages compiled into that bundle and the installed Python
runtime packages. Those packages are inventoried in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md;
their collected licence texts are still an explicit release blocker there.
